 @Soundpost-f2l  Different type of inspection process. My shop isn't huge either, but we do similar stuff to BGA type chips. Visual inspection of pad alignment and solder connections can be made on parts with exposed leads, either done manually under a microscope or done in a photo inspection machine.

For BGA and other chips that don't have exposed leads, is much more difficult to inspect without something like x-ray. With the chips we use, inspecting the paste application through the stencil is very important. Insufficient paste will cause bad connections.

From my experience digital signals like PWM signals etc. tend not to be too sensitive. The more challenging thing tends to be low voltage analog signals like those used in measuring temperature through inexpensive transistors/ thermal diodes, and for this our working range is -40c to +85c

